// Test: canaries/time
// Namespace: time (Layer 2, all hosts)
// Registry entries covered: _time_now, _time_now_seconds, _time_performance_now
// Grammar: 5.3 function calls
// Semantic: FUNC-001 bridge function resolution
// Notes: The time.* result is host-dependent — asserting a specific value is
//   impossible. The canary establishes that time.now() resolves and its
//   integer result is usable in a toString() chain — the exact pattern that
//   used to trap at wasm-function[234]:0x4bbf on clean-server (fingerprints
//   95c8a7cd TIME-TOSTRING-001, ba77c082 CODEGEN-I64-TOSTRING-OOB, 97d07985
//   COD001). Regression guard: reverting the typechecker's `time.now →
//   Integer` inference (src/typechecker/type_inference.rs) causes the MIR
//   builder to unbox the raw i32 timestamp as a boxed pointer at offset 4
//   → OOB read.
//   server.sleep + time.now in the same start block still trips a distinct
//   COM001 codegen bug (fingerprint a97c328a) unrelated to the toString
//   dispatch; that combo is left as a follow-up.
// Expected output:
//   ok

start:
	integer now = time.now()
	string _ = now.toString()
	printl("ok")
